  "chapter_notes": {
    "summary": "James 3 addresses the power of the tongue using deeply Hebrew imagery. The resen (bridle, v3) connects back to 1:25, creating a structural thread. Gehinnom (v6) is the Hebrew topographical name for the Valley of Hinnom (Josh 15:8, 2 Ki 23:10, Jer 7:31), not a Greek theological concept. Tselem Elohim (image of God, v9) quotes Genesis 1:26-27 verbatim. The botanical imagery (palms, oil, vine, figs, v12) uses native Hebrew agricultural vocabulary. Sam hamavet (poison of death, v8) is a distinctly Hebrew idiom. The wisdom from above (v17) is described with Hebrew ethical vocabulary: naki (pure), shalom (peace), nachat (gentleness), rachamim (mercies). Me'ha-adamah (from the ground, v15) echoes the Genesis adam/adamah wordplay."
  }
